WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:03.712
Vision is so natural for humans.

00:00:03.795 --> 00:00:06.798
You just open your eyes
and you understand.

00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:11.511
Vision is a very, very complicated
process, but we don't register

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.971
most of it consciously.

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:16.558
And so it's very hard for us to realize
why is it such a hard

00:00:16.558 --> 00:00:18.518
problem for the computer.

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:22.731
I try to understand
how we can make computers

00:00:22.731 --> 00:00:25.900
see and understand our visual world.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.487
For example, if I am looking at this
redwood tree,

00:00:29.487 --> 00:00:31.656
 I am not just looking at this tree.

00:00:31.656 --> 00:00:34.325
I'm looking at it through the prism

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:36.911
of what I have seen before.

00:00:36.911 --> 00:00:40.206
So how do we train computers to see?

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:42.459
A lot of the current.

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:44.419
Computer vision systems are

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:49.132
very much
about translating the visual world into a

00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:51.134
few keywords.

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:52.927
Which of course is quite limiting.

00:00:52.927 --> 00:00:55.930
Because a picture is worth
a thousand words.

00:00:56.514 --> 00:00:58.641
The field of computer vision

00:00:58.641 --> 00:01:02.270
today is undergoing a big shift.

00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:06.316
We were working really hard
for many decades.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:08.943
And we had basically nothing
to show for it.

00:01:08.943 --> 00:01:10.820
The problem turned out to be much

00:01:11.321 --> 00:01:14.699
deeper and much more exciting
than anyone imagined.

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:21.039
When people were starting to look at

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:22.248
artificial intelligence

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:26.920
in the early sixties at MIT,
they realized that vision

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.006
should be an integral
part of the story of a robot.

00:01:30.006 --> 00:01:33.426
The robot has some cameras and the robot
is going to sense its environment

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:34.928
and do actions. 

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:38.973
The vision part of this grand goal of an AI
robot was.

00:01:38.973 --> 00:01:41.976
Considered to be a summer project.

00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:44.354
The idea was that if

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:45.313
you write a smart

00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:46.272
enough algorithm,

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.275
you will teach the computer how to see.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.319
Only much later did we

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:52.779
realize that.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:56.324
what the computer needs is to connect

00:01:56.407 --> 00:01:59.661
what it's sensing with what it has seen in the past.

00:02:01.913 --> 00:02:04.582
One of my superpowers that helped me early

00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:07.585
on was that my vision is not great.

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:11.297
A lot of people I interact with every day
don't know that I have poor vision.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.424
I am able

00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:16.386
to compensate with good visual memory.

00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:20.974
I just remember a lot of things
instead of seeing them.

00:02:21.015 --> 00:02:25.562
In a sense, my brain fills
in a lot of the missing details

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:28.731
and that was very important for me early

00:02:28.731 --> 00:02:33.278
on to realize the huge importance
of large-scale visual data

00:02:33.278 --> 00:02:36.906
playing in this enterprise
of seeing and understanding.

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:38.908
That we're not seeing

00:02:38.908 --> 00:02:43.830
just with our eyes, we are seeing
with our eyes and our memory.

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:46.124
The pattern

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:48.793
of bark on the redwood tree, for example.

00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:53.423
The way it looks is defined
by so many different things:

00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:54.757
The genome of the tree, 

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.759
the weather. And it's impossible to

00:02:56.759 --> 00:03:00.305
basically write down the precise formula
which would represent it.

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:02.807
But it is possible to capture

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:06.186
a lot of examples of different trees
and different patterns of

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:09.898
bark and that we can use to model

00:03:09.898 --> 00:03:11.649
what this bark might look like.

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:16.279
Data is

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:20.116
absolutely fundamental to machine
learning in general,

00:03:20.116 --> 00:03:22.243
and computer vision in particular.

00:03:22.243 --> 00:03:24.787
Everybody is always excited
about algorithms

00:03:24.787 --> 00:03:28.082
and actually, it's the data
that's doing a lot of the heavy lifting.

00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:32.754
So one way to think about
what my lab is doing is to give data

00:03:32.754 --> 00:03:35.757
the appreciation it deserves.

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:37.634
In our lab,

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:38.760
we do many different things

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.055
connected with visual data: 

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:44.515
scene understanding, image generation

00:03:44.515 --> 00:03:48.102
and editing...
Basically, modeling visual world

00:03:48.102 --> 00:03:51.606
by creating or modifying it.

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:56.319
Our work is actually starting
to be in the real world.

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:59.781
Self-driving cars, of course, software

00:03:59.781 --> 00:04:02.951
like Adobe Photoshop, 

00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:04.535
what we call computational photography.

00:04:04.535 --> 00:04:09.624
There is a lot of computer vision that happens with the
latest smartphones from Google and Apple.

00:04:10.541 --> 00:04:12.418
So there are two basic

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:15.129
paradigms in contemporary computer vision.

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:18.007
The older and still most prominent.

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:21.010
paradigm is supervised learning.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:22.595
It's basically you get a huge amount of images, 

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:25.265
for example, or a video,

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:29.560
and then for every image,
some human is going to go and annotate

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.772
what is on that image.

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:38.444
That supervision is often
something that can introduce more bias.

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:40.154
It comes from the labels.

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:41.739
And now

00:04:41.739 --> 00:04:44.826
you have millions of these labeled images.

00:04:45.576 --> 00:04:46.077
The neural network

00:04:46.077 --> 00:04:50.164
is going to try
to learn the association

00:04:50.164 --> 00:04:52.500
between a particular image and the corresponding

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.962
label, and some of those categories
are not very meaningful.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.257
What we do in our lab is

00:04:59.257 --> 00:05:02.260
what we call self-supervised learning.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.345
We try to build models that do not

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:07.598
require this linguistic supervision -- they can understand

00:05:07.598 --> 00:05:10.310
the world from the raw data itself,

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.354
much like animals
are able to understand the world.

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:15.606
So, for example, we can have an image

00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:19.694
and then make some holes in that image
and then have the computer fill in

00:05:19.694 --> 00:05:20.570
those holes.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:24.407
Or we can try to predict in a video
what's going to happen next.

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:30.580
You get rid of the labels and so you can
get rid of a lot of the bias that is

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:31.748
baked into those labels.

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:35.710
One issue that I have been

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:37.295
thinking for a while:

00:05:37.295 --> 00:05:42.342
We, biological agents, are experiencing
the world in a continuous fashion.

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:45.762
We're going through life continuously

00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:49.098
adapting in this kind of never-ending learning loop.

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:51.142
For example, you fly

00:05:51.142 --> 00:05:54.103
into an airport
that you have never been before,

00:05:54.103 --> 00:05:54.604
and yet

00:05:54.604 --> 00:05:58.524
you're able to navigate in that airport,
even if it maybe looks very different

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:02.487
from the other airports
you're used to. 

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:06.366
For computers, this problem is more extreme
because computers are less able

00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:10.328
to generalize. In most machine
learning algorithms,

00:06:10.328 --> 00:06:12.580
we have a fixed data set.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.583
It might be a very big dataset,
you know, billions of images.

00:06:15.625 --> 00:06:19.462
We let the computer train
on all the data in this dataset.

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:23.132
And then that model is frozen. It's fixed.

00:06:23.174 --> 00:06:26.094
We go out into the real world
and we just hope

00:06:26.094 --> 00:06:30.598
somehow
we are able to perform well on this data.

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:33.101
One of the projects that

00:06:33.101 --> 00:06:36.687
we've been doing in our lab
is what we call test-time training,

00:06:36.854 --> 00:06:40.608
which is the idea
that every time we are faced with a new

00:06:40.817 --> 00:06:43.027
piece of data -- a new image, for example,

00:06:43.027 --> 00:06:46.781
we use that image to adopt our model.

00:06:47.448 --> 00:06:48.616
It's always updating.

00:06:48.616 --> 00:06:50.660
When the environment changes the model

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.079
hopefully will change with it.

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:54.956
So, for example, for self-driving cars,

00:06:54.956 --> 00:06:57.417
a lot of the self-driving
cars are based in

00:06:57.417 --> 00:07:00.628
California,
where it rarely snows, almost never.

00:07:00.628 --> 00:07:02.964
And then they go to Minnesota.

00:07:02.964 --> 00:07:04.966
There needs to be a mechanism

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:07.969
for the car to somehow adapt.

00:07:08.678 --> 00:07:12.849
Right now in our field,
we are finding things out all the time.

00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:19.105
The latest text-generative models
like Chat GPT have been very exciting for me.

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:19.814
I really should not have

00:07:19.814 --> 00:07:20.857
been surprised,

00:07:20.857 --> 00:07:23.776
but I still was very surprised

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:26.863
how good those algorithms were

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:29.949
once you give them enough
data. 

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:31.242
Huge, huge amounts of data

00:07:31.242 --> 00:07:37.331
give you these almost magical abilities
to generalize, to do analogies.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:41.169
A lot of my students have started
to get interested in

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:45.465
robotics and the connection
between robotics and computer vision.

00:07:46.007 --> 00:07:49.385
They are talking about this interaction
between the data and the algorithm

00:07:49.385 --> 00:07:52.430
and trying to figure out what is happening
on the inside.

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:55.558
It's kind of like doing neuroscience
on the computer.

00:07:56.559 --> 00:07:59.896
Hopefully that might even give us
some insights

00:07:59.896 --> 00:08:02.857
into how biological agents see the world.

00:08:05.109 --> 00:08:06.194
I think my proudest

00:08:06.194 --> 00:08:07.153
achievement has

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:11.073
veen producing the next generation.

00:08:11.073 --> 00:08:15.453
I have had absolutely wonderful
graduate students and post-docs.

00:08:16.287 --> 00:08:19.832
I feel like
this is really my biggest contribution to science.

